{"name":"Dolomite Crystal","hex":"#fee8f5","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dolomite","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An evaporite consisting of a mixed calcium and magnesium carbonate, with the chemical formula CaMg(CO3)2; it also exists as the rock dolostone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dolomite"},{"term":"crystal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A solid composed of an array of atoms or molecules possessing long-range order and arranged in a pattern which is periodic in three dimensions.","A piece of glimmering, shining mineral resembling ice or glass."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Made of crystal.","Very clear; coherent."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crystal"}],"entries":[{"title":"Dolomite (rock)","blocks":[{"text":"Dolomite (also known as dolomite rock, dolostone or dolomitic rock) is a sedimentary carbonate rock that contains a high percentage of the mineral dolomite, CaMg(CO3)2. It occurs widely, often in association with limestone and evaporites, though it is less abundant than limestone and rare in Cenozoic rock beds (beds less than about 66 million years in age). One of the first geologists to distinguish dolomite from limestone was Déodat Gratet de Dolomieu, a French mineralogist and geologist after whom it is named. He recognized and described the distinct characteristics of dolomite in the late 18th century, differentiating it from limestone."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/7092_pieskovna_Dolinka_pri_Hradisti_pod_Vratnom_dolomit.JPG/330px-7092_pieskovna_Dolinka_pri_Hradisti_pod_Vratnom_dolomit.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolomite_(rock)","wikidata":"Q1134814"},{"title":"Dolomite (mineral)","blocks":[{"text":"Dolomite is an anhydrous carbonate mineral composed of calcium magnesium carbonate, ideally CaMg(CO3)2. The term is also used for a sedimentary carbonate rock composed mostly of the mineral dolomite (see dolomite (rock)). An alternative name sometimes used for the dolomitic rock type is dolostone."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d8/Dolomite_Luzenac.jpg/330px-Dolomite_Luzenac.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolomite_(mineral)","wikidata":"Q167741"},{"title":"Crystal","blocks":[{"text":"A crystal or crystalline solid is a solid material whose constituents are arranged in a highly ordered microscopic structure, forming a crystal lattice that extends in all directions. In addition, macroscopic single crystals are usually identifiable by their geometrical shape, consisting of flat faces with specific, characteristic orientations. The scientific study of crystals and crystal formation is known as crystallography. The process of crystal formation via mechanisms of crystal growth is called crystallization or solidification."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Quartz_28.jpg/330px-Quartz_28.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crystal","wikidata":"Q43533"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Canadian_Horseshoe_Falls_with_city_of_Niagara_Falls%2C_Ontario_in_background.jpg/500px-Canadian_Horseshoe_Falls_with_city_of_Niagara_Falls%2C_Ontario_in_background.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Canadian_Horseshoe_Falls_with_city_of_Niagara_Falls%2C_Ontario_in_background.jpg/1280px-Canadian_Horseshoe_Falls_with_city_of_Niagara_Falls%2C_Ontario_in_background.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Canadian_Horseshoe_Falls_with_city_of_Niagara_Falls%2C_Ontario_in_background.jpg/500px-Canadian_Horseshoe_Falls_with_city_of_Niagara_Falls%2C_Ontario_in_background.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Erosion of dolomite over weaker shale created the Niagara Escarpment","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACanadian_Horseshoe_Falls_with_city_of_Niagara_Falls%2C_Ontario_in_background.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Sthenarocalymene_celebra_-_Arthropoda%2C_Trilobita%2C_Polymerida%2C_Calymenidae_-_Silurian_-_Ohio%2C_USA.jpg/500px-Sthenarocalymene_celebra_-_Arthropoda%2C_Trilobita%2C_Polymerida%2C_Calymenidae_-_Silurian_-_Ohio%2C_USA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Sthenarocalymene_celebra_-_Arthropoda%2C_Trilobita%2C_Polymerida%2C_Calymenidae_-_Silurian_-_Ohio%2C_USA.jpg/1280px-Sthenarocalymene_celebra_-_Arthropoda%2C_Trilobita%2C_Polymerida%2C_Calymenidae_-_Silurian_-_Ohio%2C_USA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Sthenarocalymene_celebra_-_Arthropoda%2C_Trilobita%2C_Polymerida%2C_Calymenidae_-_Silurian_-_Ohio%2C_USA.jpg/500px-Sthenarocalymene_celebra_-_Arthropoda%2C_Trilobita%2C_Polymerida%2C_Calymenidae_-_Silurian_-_Ohio%2C_USA.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Trilobite fossil preserved as an internal cast in Silurian dolomite from southwestern Ohio, US","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASthenarocalymene_celebra_-_Arthropoda%2C_Trilobita%2C_Polymerida%2C_Calymenidae_-_Silurian_-_Ohio%2C_USA.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Cirque_de_Mour%C3%A8ze%2C_H%C3%A9rault_32.jpg/500px-Cirque_de_Mour%C3%A8ze%2C_H%C3%A9rault_32.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikiped